'''''Wolffia''''' is a genus of aquatic plants with a [[cosmopolitan distribution]].<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=Wolffia Horkel ex Schleid {{!}} Plants of the World Online {{!}} Kew Science |url=http://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:331309-2 |access-date=2022-07-05 |website=Plants of the World Online |language=en}}</ref> They include the smallest [[flowering plant]]s on Earth.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |date=2009-11-25 |title=The Charms of Duckweed |url=http://www.mobot.org/jwcross/duckweed/duckweed.htm |access-date=2022-07-05 |website= |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091125213317/http://www.mobot.org/jwcross/duckweed/duckweed.htm |archive-date=25 November 2009 |url-status=dead}}</ref> Commonly called '''watermeal''' or '''rootless duckweed''',<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |title=UC/JEPS: Jepson Manual treatment for WOLFFIA |url=https://ucjeps.berkeley.edu/cgi-bin/get_JM_treatment.pl?8328,8340 |access-date=2022-07-05 |website=ucjeps.berkeley.edu}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=USDA Plants Database |url=https://plants.usda.gov/home/plantProfile?symbol=WOLFF |access-date=2022-07-05 |website=plants.usda.gov}}</ref> these [[aquatic plant]]s resemble specks of [[cornmeal]] floating on the water. They often float together in pairs or form floating mats with related plants, such as ''[[Lemna]]'' and ''[[Spirodela]]'' species.

== Description ==
''Wolffia'' are free-floating aquatic plants with [[frond]]s that are nearly spherical to cylindrical in shape and lack airspaces or veins.<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":2" /> They do not have roots.<ref name=":0" /> Their rarely seen flowers originate from a cavity on the upper surface of the frond, and each flower has one stamen and one pistil.<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":2" />

Although ''Wolffia'' can reproduce by seed, they usually use [[vegetative reproduction]].<ref name=":1" /> A mother frond has a terminal conical cavity from which it produces daughter fronds.<ref name=":0" />

== Physiology ==
The growth rate of ''Wolffia'' varies within and among species. The rates of [[photosynthesis]] and [[Respiration (physiology)|respiration]] also vary proportionately to growth rate. The fastest growth rate (in fact, the fastest growth rate of any [[flowering plant]]) is shown by a clone of ''Wolffia microscopica'', with a [[doubling time]] of 29.3 hours.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Sree |first1=K. Sowjanya |last2=Sudakaran |first2=Sailendharan |last3=Appenroth |first3=Klaus-J. |date=2015-09-11 |title=How fast can angiosperms grow? Species and clonal diversity of growth rates in the genus Wolffia (Lemnaceae) |url=https://doi.org/10.1007/s11738-015-1951-3 |journal=Acta Physiologiae Plantarum |language=en |volume=37 |issue=10 |pages=204 |doi=10.1007/s11738-015-1951-3 |bibcode=2015AcPPl..37..204S |s2cid=255376153 |issn=1861-1664|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

== As food ==
''Wolffia'' are a potential high-protein livestock food source. One species, ''W. microscopica'', is over 20% [[protein (nutrient)|protein]] by dry weight and has high content of [[essential amino acid]]s. They have historically been collected from the water and eaten as a vegetable in Asia.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Appenroth |first1=Klaus-J. |last2=Sree |first2=K. Sowjanya |last3=Böhm |first3=Volker |last4=Hammann |first4=Simon |last5=Vetter |first5=Walter |last6=Leiterer |first6=Matthias |last7=Jahreis |first7=Gerhard |date=2017-02-15 |title=Nutritional value of duckweeds (Lemnaceae) as human food |url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0308814616313565 |journal=Food Chemistry |language=en |volume=217 |pages=266–273 |doi=10.1016/j.foodchem.2016.08.116 |pmid=27664634 |issn=0308-8146|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

